 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Investors split on Huang&#x27;s future 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images Nvidia investors are rushing to CEO Jensen Huang's defense publicly, while privately expressing disappointment, after he admitted to having an affair with a purported Chinese spy, Axios' Madison Mills reports. 

 The company's stock was down about 5.5% as of midafternoon. 🤳 Pershing Square CEO Bill Ackman wrote on X that Huang (pronounced wong) "is an incredible human. ... We should all support him and his family."

 Elon Musk, replying to a post which suggested that Huang had "no bad intention," wrote : "I agree." Gavin Baker, managing partner and CIO of Atreides Management, said on X : "I simply do not believe he would ever knowingly do anything to disadvantage either America or Nvidia." One Nvidia investor told Axios that the situation is "profoundly disappointing," but added that they think Huang will keep his job.

 Dan Ives, senior managing director at Yorkville Ives, wrote in a note to clients: "Nvidia and its AI success and ambitions are heavily tied to [Huang] staying on as CEO." 🏛️ Between the lines: A newly blue Congress is looking to investigate tech execs who work closely with the Trump administration.

 One investor told Axios they're concerned about how much time Huang will have to spend in Washington as investigations unfold. 🔎 What's next: Nvidia's board has launched an independent probe, which investors worry could result in pressure for Huang to leave his post. 

